<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Amphictyonic League</h1><p class="st-meta">595 BCE – 150 CE (approx.) · capital Delphi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ancient religious association of Greek peoples that managed the sanctuary of Apollo at Delphi and repeatedly declared sacred wars that drew in the great powers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>An amphictyony was a league of neighbours sharing a sanctuary. This one began at the shrine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demeter">Demeter</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthela">Anthela</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thermopylae">Thermopylae</a> and extended its care to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a>. Its role became prominent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sacred-war">First Sacred War</a>, traditionally c. 595-585 BCE, when members destroyed the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kirrha">Kirrha</a> for abusing pilgrims and then reorganised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythian-games">Pythian Games</a> in 582 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Twelve peoples, among them the Thessalians, Phocians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a>, each sent two delegates (hieromnemones) to a council meeting twice a year at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthela">Anthela</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a>. The council looked after temple property and finances, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythian-games">Pythian Games</a> and the sacred land, and could fine or declare war on offenders. Powerful states used its votes to dress political aims as piety.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-sacred-war">Third Sacred War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a> (356-346 BCE) brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> onto the council, and the Fourth (339 BCE) led to his victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaeronea-338-bce">Chaeronea</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> dominated it in the 3rd century BCE. Augustus reorganised its membership, and it lingered as a religious body into the 2nd century CE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 595 BCE – 150 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:47.86%;width:14.83%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>595 BCE – 150 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Delphi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion (cult of Apollo and Demeter)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Delphic Amphictyony, Delphic-Pylaean Amphictyony</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-222&amp;focus=amphictyonic-league" aria-label="Amphictyonic League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5239 796 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Amphictyonic League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5559 1052L5559 1052M5559 1052L5548 987M5559 1052L5538 1049"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5559 1052h0M5548 987h0M5538 1049h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5559 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5559 1052h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Delphi. <a href="/atlas?year=-222&amp;focus=amphictyonic-league">On the atlas 